## Why This Is Asked

Real-world payments pipelines rely on idempotent, replay-safe processing across multiple services. This question probes practical test design for exactly-once-like semantics in a streaming reconciliation workflow.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency and deduplication in event-driven flows
- Exactly-once-like semantics via transactional writes
- End-to-end replay safety and offset management
- Fault injection and CI validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple idempotent ledger upsert
const processed = new Set();
function applyEvent(evt){
  if(processed.has(evt.id)) return;
  balance += evt.amount;
  processed.add(evt.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate duplicates under clock skew?
- How would you monitor and alert for replayed events?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks practical ability to design reliable, beginner-friendly tests for a time-based data pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Dependency injection for test doubles
- Time control to remove nondeterminism
- In-memory mocks vs real services
- Flake detection and CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest example with in-memory queue and fixed time
const { processNext } = require('../src/worker');
const InMemoryQueue = require('../src/mocks').InMemoryQueue;
const redisApi = require('../src/redis');

jest.mock('../src/redis');

test('processNext deterministic time', () => {
  const queue = new InMemoryQueue(['action1','action2']);
  redisApi.getQueue.mockReturnValue(queue);
  jest.useFakeTimers('modern');
  jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2026-01-01T12:00:00Z'));
  processNext();
  expect(queue.processed).toBe(true);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multiple worker instances with separate queues?
- What metrics would indicate flaky tests and how would you triage them?

